{
  "chalk": 1,
  "name": "03-mutual-exclusion.chalk",
  "cells": [
    {
      "src": "Mutual exclusion",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!goal]\n> Build a lock that lets at most one thread into its critical section, find what is wrong with the first attempts from the checker's traces, and prove the fix.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The lost update needs both threads between a read and a write. Make that stretch a **critical section** that at most one thread may be in. The obvious lock: look at it, and if it is free, take it. Two threads, one lock:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Race = system(\n  var p in {idle, checked, crit}\n  var q in {idle, checked, crit}\n  var lock in bool\n  init p = idle ∧ q = idle ∧ lock = false\n  action pcheck when p = idle ∧ lock = false do p := checked\n  action pset when p = checked do lock := true, p := crit\n  action pexit when p = crit do p := idle, lock := false\n  action qcheck when q = idle ∧ lock = false do q := checked\n  action qset when q = checked do lock := true, q := crit\n  action qexit when q = crit do q := idle, lock := false\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!try]\n> Can both threads get in? If so, in how few steps?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "invariant(Race, ¬(p = crit ∧ q = crit))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "Both look while the lock is free, then both take it: **check, then act**, with a gap between. It is lesson 1's bug again, one level up: reading the lock and writing it are separate steps.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Closing the gap",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Make the check and the take one action, as a compare-and-set instruction or a database's conditional write does:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Fix = system(\n  var p in {idle, crit}\n  var q in {idle, crit}\n  var lock in bool\n  init p = idle ∧ q = idle ∧ lock = false\n  action penter when p = idle ∧ lock = false do lock := true, p := crit\n  action pexit when p = crit do p := idle, lock := false\n  action qenter when q = idle ∧ lock = false do lock := true, q := crit\n  action qexit when q = crit do q := idle, lock := false\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "invariant(Fix, ¬(p = crit ∧ q = crit))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The checker has tried all 3 reachable states. For a proof, lesson 2's induction: is \"not both inside\" inductive?",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "inductive(Fix, ¬(p = crit ∧ q = crit))",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Not by itself: from an unreachable state where `q` is inside without the lock, `p` can enter. What rules it out is why the lock works: whoever is inside holds the lock. Strengthened with that, it is inductive:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "inductive(Fix, (p = crit → lock = true) ∧ (q = crit → lock = true) ∧ ¬(p = crit ∧ q = crit))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Without an atomic step",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Can two threads exclude each other with nothing but ordinary reads and writes? Give each a flag. Raising it first, then looking, closes the gap: a thread only looks once its own flag is up.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Flags = system(\n  var p in {idle, want, crit}\n  var q in {idle, want, crit}\n  var fp in bool\n  var fq in bool\n  init p = idle ∧ q = idle ∧ fp = false ∧ fq = false\n  action pwant when p = idle do fp := true, p := want\n  action penter when p = want ∧ fq = false do p := crit\n  action pexit when p = crit do fp := false, p := idle\n  action qwant when q = idle do fq := true, q := want\n  action qenter when q = want ∧ fp = false do q := crit\n  action qexit when q = crit do fq := false, q := idle\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "invariant(Flags, ¬(p = crit ∧ q = crit))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "deadlock(Flags)",
      "showWork": true,
      "label": null,
      "stepwise": 0
    },
    {
      "src": "Safe, but both can raise their flags and then wait for each other for ever: a **deadlock**. The trace says what is missing: a tie-breaker for when both want in. Add a shared `turn`. After raising its flag, each thread gives the turn to the other, and enters once the other's flag is down or the turn has come back to it. Whoever wrote `turn` last waits. This is **Peterson's algorithm**:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Peterson = system(\n  var p in {idle, flag, wait, crit}\n  var q in {idle, flag, wait, crit}\n  var fp in bool\n  var fq in bool\n  var turn in {P, Q}\n  init p = idle ∧ q = idle ∧ fp = false ∧ fq = false ∧ turn = P\n  action pflag when p = idle do fp := true, p := flag\n  action pyield when p = flag do turn := Q, p := wait\n  action penter when p = wait ∧ (fq = false ∨ turn = P) do p := crit\n  action pexit when p = crit do fp := false, p := idle\n  action qflag when q = idle do fq := true, q := flag\n  action qyield when q = flag do turn := P, q := wait\n  action qenter when q = wait ∧ (fp = false ∨ turn = Q) do q := crit\n  action qexit when q = crit do fq := false, q := idle\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "invariant(Peterson, ¬(p = crit ∧ q = crit))",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "deadlock(Peterson)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In TLA+",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The atomic lock in TLA+, for the TLC model checker:\n\n```\nVARIABLES p, q, lock\nInit == p = \"idle\" /\\ q = \"idle\" /\\ lock = FALSE\nPEnter == p = \"idle\" /\\ lock = FALSE /\\ p' = \"crit\" /\\ lock' = TRUE /\\ UNCHANGED q\nPExit  == p = \"crit\" /\\ p' = \"idle\" /\\ lock' = FALSE /\\ UNCHANGED q\nQEnter == q = \"idle\" /\\ lock = FALSE /\\ q' = \"crit\" /\\ lock' = TRUE /\\ UNCHANGED p\nQExit  == q = \"crit\" /\\ q' = \"idle\" /\\ lock' = FALSE /\\ UNCHANGED p\nNext == PEnter \\/ PExit \\/ QEnter \\/ QExit\nMutualExclusion == ~(p = \"crit\" /\\ q = \"crit\")\n```\n\nThe primed variables are the next state's; `UNCHANGED` says what an action leaves alone, which a system cell leaves implicit.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "In Lean",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "The atomic lock again, and lesson 2's induction with the strengthened invariant:",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "/-- Two processes and a lock: each is in its critical section or not. -/\nstructure Mutex where\n  p : Bool\n  q : Bool\n  lock : Bool\n  deriving DecidableEq, Repr\n\ndef mutex : TS Mutex where\n  init s := s = ⟨false, false, false⟩\n  step s t :=\n    (s.p = false ∧ s.lock = false ∧ t = { s with p := true, lock := true }) ∨\n    (s.p = true ∧ t = { s with p := false, lock := false }) ∨\n    (s.q = false ∧ s.lock = false ∧ t = { s with q := true, lock := true }) ∨\n    (s.q = true ∧ t = { s with q := false, lock := false })\n\n/-- The strengthened invariant: whoever is inside holds the lock, and not both are inside. -/\ndef MutexInv (s : Mutex) : Prop :=\n  (s.p = true → s.lock = true) ∧ (s.q = true → s.lock = true) ∧ ¬(s.p = true ∧ s.q = true)",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem mutexInv_init : MutexInv ⟨false, false, false⟩ := by",
      "type": "exercise",
      "lean": true,
      "prompt": "Prove that the strengthened invariant holds initially.",
      "leanSolution": "  simp [MutexInv]",
      "hints": [
        "Unfold `MutexInv`: every part is about `false = true`."
      ],
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "theorem mutex_safe : ∀ s, Reachable mutex s → ¬(s.p = true ∧ s.q = true) := by\n  intro s h\n  have : MutexInv s := by\n    apply invariant_of_inductive mutex MutexInv _ _ s h\n    · intro s hs; simp [mutex] at hs; subst hs; exact mutexInv_init\n    · intro s t hI hst\n      obtain ⟨hp, hq, hpq⟩ := hI\n      rcases s with ⟨p, q, l⟩\n      simp only [mutex] at hst\n      rcases hst with ⟨h1, h2, rfl⟩ | ⟨h1, rfl⟩ | ⟨h1, h2, rfl⟩ | ⟨h1, rfl⟩ <;>\n        simp_all [MutexInv] <;> cases p <;> cases q <;> simp_all\n  exact this.2.2",
      "type": "lean",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Exercises",
      "type": "section",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Peterson's algorithm with its two writes the other way round: give away the turn first, then raise the flag.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Swapped = system(\n  var p in {idle, flag, wait, crit}\n  var q in {idle, flag, wait, crit}\n  var fp in bool\n  var fq in bool\n  var turn in {P, Q}\n  init p = idle ∧ q = idle ∧ fp = false ∧ fq = false ∧ turn = P\n  action pyield when p = idle do turn := Q, p := flag\n  action pflag when p = flag do fp := true, p := wait\n  action penter when p = wait ∧ (fq = false ∨ turn = P) do p := crit\n  action pexit when p = crit do fp := false, p := idle\n  action qyield when q = idle do turn := P, q := flag\n  action qflag when q = flag do fq := true, q := wait\n  action qenter when q = wait ∧ (fp = false ∨ turn = Q) do q := crit\n  action qexit when q = crit do fq := false, q := idle\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "reach(Swapped, p = crit ∧ q = crit)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Can both threads be inside at once in `Swapped`? Answer `true` or `false`.",
      "hints": [
        "Let `p` give away the turn, then `q`: now the turn is `p`'s. Can `q` get in before `p`'s flag goes up?"
      ],
      "hideQuestion": true,
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "Strict alternation: one shared `turn`, and each thread hands it over on the way out.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "let Turn = system(\n  var p in {idle, crit}\n  var q in {idle, crit}\n  var turn in {P, Q}\n  init p = idle ∧ q = idle ∧ turn = P\n  action penter when p = idle ∧ turn = P do p := crit\n  action pexit when p = crit do p := idle, turn := Q\n  action qenter when q = idle ∧ turn = Q do q := crit\n  action qexit when q = crit do q := idle, turn := P\n)",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "invariant(Turn, ¬(p = crit ∧ q = crit))",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Is `Turn` safe: never both inside?",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "deadlock(Turn)",
      "type": "exercise",
      "prompt": "Can `Turn` deadlock?",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "> [!summary]\n> A lock that checks and then takes is a race, because the check and the take are separate steps; the checker finds the interleaving. One fix makes them one step, and an inductive invariant (\"inside means holding the lock\") proves it. Without an atomic step, flags alone deadlock, and Peterson's turn breaks the tie; the order of its two writes matters.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    },
    {
      "src": "`Turn` passes both checks, yet `p` cannot go in twice in a row: if `q` stops wanting in, `p` waits for ever. No state is bad; a good thing just never happens. Saying that needs a second kind of property, lesson 5's. Before that, the lock as programs keep it: one per key, in a table, with calls that each promise to be one step.",
      "type": "markdown",
      "showWork": false,
      "label": null
    }
  ],
  "scenes": []
}
